{"name":"Plaster Cast","hex":"#e1eaec","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"plaster cast","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cast made of a stiff material (often plaster of Paris and gauze) in order to immobilize a broken bone for the period of healing.","A copy of a piece of art or other object cast in plaster."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/plaster_cast"}],"entries":[{"title":"Plaster cast","blocks":[{"text":"A plaster cast is a copy made in plaster of another 3-dimensional form. The original from which the cast is taken may be a sculpture, building, a face, a pregnant belly, a fossil or other remains such as fresh or fossilised footprints – particularly in palaeontology."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/05/HoudonWashingtonNPG.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plaster_cast","wikidata":"Q3325510"},{"title":"Plaster","blocks":[{"text":"Plaster is a building material typically used for cladding on interior walls, pool interiors, ceilings and for moulding and casting decorative elements. In English, \"plaster\" usually means a material used for the interiors of buildings, while \"render\" commonly refers to external applications. The term stucco refers to a physically different mixture commonly used for exterior building wall cladding."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Fontainebleau_escalier_roi5.jpg/330px-Fontainebleau_escalier_roi5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plaster","wikidata":"Q274988"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Face_casting.jpg/500px-Face_casting.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Face_casting.jpg/1280px-Face_casting.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Face_casting.jpg/500px-Face_casting.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Face casting process, with plastered bandage","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFace_casting.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/The_Royal_Academicians_in_General_Assembly.png/500px-The_Royal_Academicians_in_General_Assembly.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/The_Royal_Academicians_in_General_Assembly.png/500px-The_Royal_Academicians_in_General_Assembly.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Royal Academicians in General Assembly by Henry Singleton. A number of casts of classical statues are on displays behind the artists.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Royal_Academicians_in_General_Assembly.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/This_room_is_full_of_plaster_cast_copies_of_famous_world_statues%2C_sculptures_and_monuments.jpg/500px-This_room_is_full_of_plaster_cast_copies_of_famous_world_statues%2C_sculptures_and_monuments.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/This_room_is_full_of_plaster_cast_copies_of_famous_world_statues%2C_sculptures_and_monuments.jpg/500px-This_room_is_full_of_plaster_cast_copies_of_famous_world_statues%2C_sculptures_and_monuments.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The West Court of the Cast Courts of the Victoria and Albert Museum","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThis_room_is_full_of_plaster_cast_copies_of_famous_world_statues%2C_sculptures_and_monuments.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia